# Give a port that appears after boot is under way the same stable address the
# cold-plug sweep gives the ones that were already there. mica-mac.service is a
# Type=oneshot with a single pass over /sys/class/net/eth*: a port that
# registers after that pass was never assigned anything and kept the kernel's
# random address, which is different on every boot. On the first hardware boot
# (2026-09-08) eth1 came up carrying be:26:d1:8c:b0:2a -- no 02: prefix, so
# nothing in Mica OS had written it -- while both NICs registered at 11.67 s, four
# and a half milliseconds apart. A one-shot cannot cover that and a per-device
# rule does not have to.
#
# RUN+= is executed after every rule of this event has been processed, not at
# this line: udev-worker.c runs udev_event_execute_rules() and then
# udev_event_execute_run(). Two consequences, and both matter here.
#
#   - It cannot beat 80-net-setup-link.rules, whatever number this file
#     carries. That is what 60-mica-mac-stable.link is for.
#   - It is still ordered before anything configures the interface, which is
#     what mica-mac.service's Before=network-pre.target buys for the ports that
#     exist early. The udev database is written after RUN and the event is
#     broadcast to libudev listeners after that, so systemd-networkd cannot see
#     the link as initialised until this program has returned.
#
# KERNEL=="eth*" is a pre-filter, so that udev does not spawn a process for lo,
# can0, wlan0 and the gadget port. hwinit-mac applies the same scope itself.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="net", KERNEL=="eth*", RUN+="/usr/lib/mica/hwinit-mac %k"
